<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="plugin_stereo_chain">

  <!-- Proposal 08 M3: a 2-in/2-out plugin in the signal path of a stereo track.
       This is the headless gate for the two defects M3 closed:

         * twPluginInsert::freezePage used to write INTERLEAVED stereo into a
           page the engine reads as MONO (twOutputPage is one mono channel);
         * a 2-in plugin only ever got bus audio on input 0 and SILENCE on
           input 1, because STrack built one twPluginChain per bus with
           nBusses == 1 and twPluginChain::rebuildWiring() only wired
           port < nBusses_.

       The plugin is the in-repo CLAP fixture's second entry point,
       tw.test.clap.stereoskew (2 in / 2 out). Its DEFAULT behaviour — all a qxa
       script can reach, since setting a parameter or restoring a state chunk is
       M5/M4 work — is deliberately CROSS-CHANNEL:

           out[0] = in[0] * 0.5 + in[1]
           out[1] = in[1] * 0.5

       A track feeds both buses the same mono material, so a correct chain puts
       channel 0 at 1.5x the unprocessed level. The three outcomes are far apart:

           1.5x  correct  (both buses wired, plugin in the path)
           1.0x  the plugin is not in the signal path at all
           0.5x  input 1 is silent  <- the pre-M3 bug

       NOTE on channel 1. The offline render still collapses the graph's buses to
       ONE mono page and duplicates it (tw303a/render/src/render_session.cc:
       "bufR[i] = sample;  // Duplicate to stereo (temporary; proper
       multi-channel TBD)"), and playback does the same. So bus 1's audio cannot
       reach a file today and the file's two channels are equal BY CONSTRUCTION —
       which is why the cross-channel term above exists, and why the channel-1
       band below is wide. When the sink becomes genuinely multi-channel, tighten
       channel 1 to [0.030, 0.037] (0.5x) and channel 0 stays as it is. That
       distinctness IS gated today, at engine level, by plugins_test's
       "the two buses are genuinely different audio".

       path="twtestclap.clap" is relative: SInsertPluginAction resolves it
       against this .qxa's directory first and then against the application
       directory, and the build copies the fixture next to the binary — so it
       works whatever the build directory is called. -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <insert-plugin trackPath="/mixer/0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.stereoskew"
                   name="Smaragd Test Stereo Skew" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="2" nOut="2"/>

    <render filename="plugin_stereo_chain.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4.5"/>

    <!-- The load-bearing assertion. Unprocessed, this second of the sawtooth
         renders at RMS ~0.0667; through the fixture it must be ~0.1000. The band
         excludes both 0.0667 (no plugin) and 0.0333 (silent input 1). -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="plugin_stereo_chain.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.090" maxRms="0.110"/>

    <!-- Channel 1 must carry real audio, whether it is bus 1 (0.5x, once the
         sink is multi-channel) or today's duplicate of bus 0 (1.5x). -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="plugin_stereo_chain.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.028" maxRms="0.110"/>

    <!-- A quiet stretch late in the sawtooth, to prove the relation holds across
         pages (a 65536-frame page boundary falls inside the clip, so this also
         exercises the processor's page-continuity bookkeeping). -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="plugin_stereo_chain.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.001" maxRms="0.900"/>

    <!-- PROPOSAL 36 AC B4.3, at the track's ROOT COMPONENT rather than in the
         rendered file. The comment above says the file's two channels are equal
         BY CONSTRUCTION and that the distinctness is gated at engine level in
         plugins_test; B4 makes it assertable from a script for the first time,
         because the whole track path is now one N-channel wire and the insert
         renders every channel in ONE process() call.

         The fixture is cross-channel by design:
             out[0] = in[0] * 0.5 + in[1]
             out[1] = in[1] * 0.5
         Both inputs carry the same sawtooth here, so channel 0 leaves at 1.5x
         and channel 1 at 0.5x — a ratio of 3, which is what the level
         discriminator below measures. All three failure modes are excluded: a
         silent input 1 gives 0.5x/0.5x (delta 0, rejected), a plugin outside the
         path gives 1x/1x (delta 0, rejected), and a per-channel loop over a
         cursor-bearing render would displace channel 1 by a page (caught by
         asserting at two positions).

         driver="pull" deliberately: this is the seam a wide insert uses to
         reach its upstream page, and it is the one the plugin layer's own
         contract (invariant 13's lock rule) is written about. -->
    <assert-track-channels trackPath="0" position="0" expectChannels="2"
                           channelA="0" channelB="1" driver="pull"
                           minRms="0.05" minRmsDelta="0.02" minDiffRms="0.02"/>
    <assert-track-channels trackPath="0" position="65536" expectChannels="2"
                           channelA="0" channelB="1" driver="pull"
                           minRms="0.02" minRmsDelta="0.01" minDiffRms="0.01"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
